# NestedText v3 - $ARGUMENTS remainder transmigration, first of the deferred expansions (measured 2026-09-16)
# Source: D:\CLAUDE_CODE_COMMANDER\artifacts\_sweep\byproducts\$ARGUMENTS_VARIANT_EXAMPLES.md sections 3-16 (lines 81-829).
# arguments.nt holds the skeleton (1-80); the seven part-files hold the
# variants; this file holds the shell-semantics mechanisms distilled, not
# the tutorial copied. Ordinal: okto, next after hepta.

quoting-discipline:
    > Unquoted $@ word-splits: three words become one or one becomes
    > three depending on quoting, never on intent. "$@" preserves arity,
    > $@ destroys it. The trust boundary's CDATA rule is this discipline
    > restated: user-args arrive quoted whole, never split by the shell.

expansion-control:
    > A heredoc delimiter chooses expansion: <<EOF expands, <<'EOF' and
    > <<"EOF" and <<\EOF do not, <<-EOF expands and strips tabs. Selective
    > escape (\$@) splits one heredoc into literal and expanded spans.
    > Nested heredocs rebind: inner $@ is the inner shell's, and outer
    > values cross only by explicit "$@" handoff. Cache writes use quoted
    > delimiters for the same reason: no expansion crosses a trust line
    > unless the writer typed the escape.

scope-and-inheritance:
    > Functions inherit $@ from the call site; nested functions inherit
    > down; shift inside a function consumes the function's copy.
    > Subshells inherit, pipelines do not: a pipeline stage sees piped
    > data, never $@, and values cross by env or explicit vars. Arrays
    > hold "$@" whole (store, slice, length); loops and while-shift parse
    > idiomatically; conditionals test membership and shape.

eval-exec-source-trap:
    > eval "$@" re-parses and injects: the same words run twice, once as
    > data and once as code, which is why tool-result is data and never
    > re-executed. exec "$@" replaces the shell with its first word.
    > source runs a file in the current shell, inheriting everything.
    > trap handlers see the $@ of their registration time, not of the
    > signal. xargs and mapfile re-chunk: one arg per line or one array.

polyglot-generation:
    > One arg vector generates YAML, NestedText, Markdown callouts, JSON,
    > TOML, Terraform and the five-layer stack, each through a heredoc
    > with the delimiter chosen per section above. Generation is
    > delimiter discipline plus quoting discipline, nothing else.

injection-vectors:
    > Unquoted $@ splits words; eval "$@" injects commands; unquoted
    > heredoc $@ injects YAML and NestedText (PyYAML RCE shape); set --
    > redefines $@ from untrusted input. Safeties in order: quote, never
    > eval, quote the delimiter, sanitize before interpolation, validate
    > before set. Five vectors, five safeties, each pair a trust rule the
    > command trust boundaries already state.

release-binding:
    > Stream 1 (trust): every vector above is a case the four trust
    > channels (user-args, tool-result, file-ref, ask-answer) close by
    > construction; the tutorial is the evidence the boundary answers.
    > Stream 4 (forms): heredoc delimiter choice is the shell half of the
    > quoted-vs-unquoted form discipline cc-form declares.
